Contents of https://github.com/dotnet/docs/issues/54359:
### Description

Starting in .NET 11, `CborReader` and `CborWriter` enforce a maximum nesting depth by default. `CborReader` limits nesting depth to 64, and `CborWriter` limits nesting depth to 1000, matching the defaults used by `Utf8JsonReader` and `Utf8JsonWriter`. Reading or writing CBOR data that exceeds the configured maximum depth now throws an exception.

PR: [dotnet/runtime#129273](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/129273)

### Version

.NET 11 Preview 5

### Previous behavior

`CborReader` and `CborWriter` had no maximum nesting depth limit. Reading or writing arbitrarily nested CBOR data structures succeeded without restriction.

// Deeply nested CBOR data (e.g., 100 levels of nested arrays)
var reader \= new CborReader(deeplyNestedBuffer);

for (int i \= 0; i < 100; i++)
{
    reader.ReadStartArray(); // Succeeded at any depth
}

var writer \= new CborWriter();

for (int i \= 0; i < 2000; i++)
{
    writer.WriteStartArray(1); // Succeeded at any depth
}

### New behavior

`CborReader` throws `CborContentException` when reading a container (array, map, or indefinite-length string) that would exceed the maximum allowed depth (default: 64). `CborWriter` throws `InvalidOperationException` when writing a container that would exceed the maximum allowed depth (default: 1000).

// Throws CborContentException when nesting depth exceeds 64
var reader \= new CborReader(deeplyNestedBuffer);

for (int i \= 0; i < 65; i++)
{
    reader.ReadStartArray(); // Throws CborContentException on the 65th call
}

// Throws InvalidOperationException when nesting depth exceeds 1000
var writer \= new CborWriter();

for (int i \= 0; i < 1001; i++)
{
    writer.WriteStartArray(1); // Throws InvalidOperationException on the 1001st call
}

To configure a higher maximum depth, use the new `CborReaderOptions` or `CborWriterOptions` types:

var options \= new CborReaderOptions { MaxDepth \= 256 };
var reader \= new CborReader(data, options);

var writerOptions \= new CborWriterOptions { MaxDepth \= 2000 };
var writer \= new CborWriter(writerOptions);

### Type of breaking change

*   **Behavioral change**: Existing binaries might behave differently at run time.

### Reason for change

Deeply nested CBOR data can cause excessive memory consumption and execution time. Skipping over a deeply nested array or map requires processing every element in the structure, which can use a surprising amount of memory. This change is consistent with the behavior of `Utf8JsonReader` and `Utf8JsonWriter`.

### Recommended action

If your application legitimately processes CBOR data nested more deeply than the defaults allow (64 levels for reading, 1000 levels for writing), use the new options types to specify a larger limit:

// For reading CBOR data nested more than 64 levels deep
var options \= new CborReaderOptions { MaxDepth \= 256 };
var reader \= new CborReader(data, options);

// For writing CBOR data nested more than 1000 levels deep
var writerOptions \= new CborWriterOptions { MaxDepth \= 2000 };
var writer \= new CborWriter(writerOptions);

Note that unlike `Utf8JsonReader`/`Utf8JsonWriter`, setting `MaxDepth = 0` in CBOR means _no nesting is allowed_ (not "use the runtime default"). To use the runtime default, set `MaxDepth = -1` or omit the property when constructing `CborReaderOptions`/`CborWriterOptions`.

There is no AppContext switch to restore the previous unlimited-depth behavior.

### Feature area

Core .NET libraries

### Affected APIs

*   `System.Formats.Cbor.CborReader.ReadStartArray()`
*   `System.Formats.Cbor.CborReader.ReadStartMap()`
*   `System.Formats.Cbor.CborReader.ReadStartIndefiniteLengthByteString()`
*   `System.Formats.Cbor.CborReader.ReadStartIndefiniteLengthTextString()`
*   `System.Formats.Cbor.CborWriter.WriteStartArray(int)` and `CborWriter.WriteStartIndefiniteLengthArray()`
*   `System.Formats.Cbor.CborWriter.WriteStartMap(int?)` and `CborWriter.WriteStartIndefiniteLengthMap()`

* * *

